Given a set \( S \) with \( |S| = 35 \) and a set \( F \) of mappings from \( S \) to itself, we need to find the least positive integer \( m \) such that if \( F \) satisfies property \( P(2019) \), then it also satisfies property \( P(m) \).

To determine this, we consider the sequence of mappings as functions \( f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_k \) such that for any \( x, y \in S \), there exist \( f_1, f_2, \ldots, f_k \) (not necessarily different) such that \( f_k(f_{k-1}(\cdots (f_1(x)) \cdots )) = f_k(f_{k-1}(\cdots (f_1(y)) \cdots )) \). We aim to find the minimal \( m \) such that if \( F \) satisfies \( P(2019) \), then it also satisfies \( P(m) \).

We define \( g_i(x) = f_i(f_{i-1}(\cdots (f_1(x)) \cdots )) \) with \( g_0(x) = x \). Let \( A_i \) be the unordered pair \( (g_i(x), g_i(y)) \). The key claim is that \( A_0, A_1, \ldots, A_k \) are all distinct, and \( A_k \) is the only pair consisting of two equal elements. If there exist two equal pairs \( A_i \) and \( A_j \) (where \( i < j \)), we can use the functions \( f_k, f_{k-1}, \ldots, f_{j+1}, f_i, f_{i-1}, \ldots, f_1 \) instead to obtain equal final values, contradicting the assumption that \( f_k, f_{k-1}, \ldots, f_1 \) is a minimal length sequence.

